## if and else

```javascript
const temperature = 18;
if (temperature < 0) {
  console.log("Freezing");
} else if (temperature < 15) {
  console.log("Cold");
} else {
  console.log("Mild or warm");
}
```

Conditions go in parentheses, blocks in braces. Braces are optional for a single statement, but always writing them avoids a classic bug when a second line is added later.

The **conditional operator** chooses between two values:

```javascript
const age = 20;
const label = age >= 18 ? "adult" : "minor";
console.log(label);
```

## switch

`switch` compares one value against several cases with `===`:

```javascript
function dayType(day) {
  switch (day) {
    case "Sat":
    case "Sun":
      return "weekend";
    case "Mon":
      return "start of the week";
    default:
      return "weekday";
  }
}
console.log(dayType("Sun"), dayType("Wed"));
```

Without `return` or `break`, execution **falls through** into the next case. That is useful for grouping cases (`"Sat"` and `"Sun"` above) and a bug when forgotten.

## for loops

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {                // counting
  console.log("round", i);
}

const fruits = ["apple", "pear", "plum"];
for (const fruit of fruits) {                 // the VALUES of an array (or string, Map, Set...)
  console.log(fruit);
}

const scores = { ada: 95, grace: 88 };
for (const key in scores) {                   // the KEYS of an object
  console.log(key, scores[key]);
}
```

Use `for...of` for arrays and strings; `for...in` is for object keys only (on arrays it gives the indexes as strings, which is rarely what you want). To get indexes with `for...of`: `for (const [i, fruit] of fruits.entries())`.

## while and do...while

```javascript
let n = 27;
let steps = 0;
while (n !== 1) {
  n = n % 2 === 0 ? n / 2 : 3 * n + 1;
  steps++;
}
console.log(steps);

let attempt = 0;
do {
  attempt++;
} while (attempt < 3);                        // the body runs at least once
console.log(attempt);
```

## break and continue

`break` leaves the loop; `continue` skips to the next round. In nested loops, a **label** says which loop to leave:

```javascript
outer: for (let row = 0; row < 3; row++) {
  for (let col = 0; col < 3; col++) {
    if (row * col === 2) {
      console.log("found at", row, col);
      break outer;
    }
  }
}
```

## Check yourself
```quiz
? What is the difference between `for...of` and `for...in`?
- `for...of` gives the keys of an object; `for...in` gives the values of an array
+ `for...of` gives the values of an array (any iterable); `for...in` gives object keys
- `for...of` counts with an index; `for...in` walks the values of any array
- There is none: both give the values of an array in order
> Use `for...of` for arrays and strings; `for...in` is for object keys (on an array it gives the indexes as strings).

? What happens when a `switch` case has no `break` or `return`?
- The switch ends after that case anyway
- JavaScript reports a syntax error
- The `default` case runs next
+ Execution falls through into the next case
> Without break or return, execution carries on into the following case's code. That is handy for grouping cases and a bug when forgotten.

? How many times does the body of `do { } while (false);` run?
+ 1
- 0
- forever
- it is a syntax error
> A do...while loop checks its condition after the body, so the body always runs at least once.
```
